Une SP = bloc T-SQL compilé stocké dans la DB. Avantages : appelable par nom, params typés, sécurisée contre SQL injection, planifiable, auditable.

```
CREATE OR ALTER PROCEDURE sp_CreateBatch
    @ProductCode NVARCHAR(20),
    @Quantity_Kg DECIMAL(10,2),
    @OperatorId INT,
    @BatchId INT OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;
    DECLARE @ProductId INT;

    SELECT @ProductId = ProductId
    FROM Products WHERE ProductCode = @ProductCode;

    IF @ProductId IS NULL BEGIN
        RAISERROR('Product % not found', 16, 1, @ProductCode);
        RETURN;
    END

    IF @Quantity_Kg <= 0 BEGIN
        RAISERROR('Quantity must be > 0', 16, 1);
        RETURN;
    END

    INSERT INTO Batches (ProductId, StartTime, PlannedQuantity_Kg,
                        OperatorId, Status)
    VALUES (@ProductId, GETDATE(), @Quantity_Kg, @OperatorId, 'Started');

    SET @BatchId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();

    INSERT INTO AuditLog (Timestamp, UserId, Action, Details)
    VALUES (GETDATE(), @OperatorId, 'CreateBatch',
            'BatchId=' + CAST(@BatchId AS NVARCHAR));
END

-- Appel
DECLARE @NewBatchId INT;
EXEC sp_CreateBatch @ProductCode='YAG-STR', @Quantity_Kg=500,
                    @OperatorId=42, @BatchId=@NewBatchId OUTPUT;
```

**À retenir :** SP = API métier sur la BDD. App appelle sp_CreateBatch(...), pas de SQL direct. Sécurisé + auditable + évolutif.
